<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomObject xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        WS5 (2026-08-30) — Broker Protection runtime configuration, moved out of Apex
        constants per the user's explicit GATE A decision A1: "make the window configurable
        as asked, and accept the retroactive-release risk", WITH the required mitigation
        (see Protection_Window_Days__c and Lookback_Days__c field comments, and
        PropertyMatchingService.cls's own header, for the full risk this accepts).

        ═══ ONE TYPE, NOT TWO — DECISION AND JUSTIFICATION ═══
        This bundles the numeric knobs (window/lookback/confidence/model/temperature/
        max_tokens) AND the ~20KB prompt body into ONE type with ONE record
        (DeveloperName = Default), rather than splitting config from prompt into two types.
        Both shapes are defensible; one was chosen for three reasons:
          1. agent-output/design-requirements.md §2/WS5 names BrokerProtectionConfig.cls as
             "a single accessor with a static cache" reading one CMDT — matching that
             shape needs one type, one record, one cache entry, not two coordinated reads.
          2. The DEPLOY ORDERING CONSTRAINT the same section pins is: "__mdt type -> RUN
             THE APEX LOADER -> only then the two BP_Expiry formula fields" (singular
             "the record"). Two types would mean two loader runs and two existence checks
             before Lead.BP_Expiry__c / Opportunity.BP_Expiry__c can safely deploy, for no
             offsetting benefit — both types would still need to exist before those
             formulas, so splitting buys no parallelism.
          3. The §6 admin prompt this build follows literally names
             "Broker_Protection_Config__mdt + 8 fields" as one type.
        The counter-argument (separating the high-risk 19KB prompt text, edited by
        developers doing prompt engineering, from the routine numeric knobs an admin tunes
        in Setup, so the two are never visible on the same edit screen and could later get
        different customMetadataTypeAccesses grants) is real and was weighed — but it is a
        PERMISSION-SET-level concern, and this task is explicitly forbidden from touching
        permission sets (see the field-level FLS handoff instead). If that separation is
        ever wanted, splitting Extraction_Prompt__c into its own type later is a clean,
        additive move (new type + new field + repoint the one accessor call site) — nothing
        here forecloses it.

        ═══ RECORDS ARE NOT DEPLOYABLE HERE ═══
        .forceignore line 16 excludes **/customMetadata/** in this org (file-based CMDT
        record deploy throws UNKNOWN_EXCEPTION here — same trap the Transaction task-groups
        build hit). This file (the TYPE) and its fields/*.field-meta.xml files ARE deployed
        normally; the single Default RECORD is loaded by an Apex script
        (scripts/load-broker-protection-config.apex, not yet written — the developer/
        technical-architect step that consumes this handoff owns it), following the exact
        pattern of scripts/load-task-group-defs.apex, scripts/load-onboarding-task-defs.apex
        and scripts/load-transaction-task-defs.apex in this same folder.

        See agent-output/design-requirements.md §0/GATE A and §2/WS5 for the full workstream
        context, and this build's own handoff (reported by the invoking agent) for the exact
        record values the loader must write.
    -->
    <label>Broker Protection Config</label>
    <pluralLabel>Broker Protection Configs</pluralLabel>
    <visibility>Public</visibility>
</CustomObject>
